---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="AI Engineering — Viv" description="Viv's approach to AI-augmented software engineering, informed by 25+ years of craft.">
  <Navigation />

  <main class="pt-24 pb-32">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">
      <a href="/" class="text-sm text-accent-functional hover:text-accent-light transition-colors mb-8 inline-block">&larr; Back to main</a>

      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-accent mb-4">AI Engineering</h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-tertiary mb-16">Last updated: February 2026</p>

      {/* Philosophy */}
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary mb-4">Philosophy</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          After 25+ years of building software, AI is the most significant shift in how the craft is practised. But the insight isn't that AI replaces engineers. It's that managing AI agents effectively requires the same skills as managing human engineering teams: scoping, planning, TDD, pairing, code review, and iterating in small increments.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          XP practices aren't obsolete. They're more relevant than ever. The same discipline that makes a senior engineer effective leading a team of humans is what makes them effective orchestrating a team of AI agents.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* How I Work with AI */}
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary mb-4">How I Work with AI</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          AI is a core part of the daily engineering workflow, not an experiment or a side project. The approach is practical: the same engineering discipline you'd apply to any team member applies to AI collaborators. Clear scoping, well-defined tasks, test-driven development, and iterative feedback loops.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The deeper thinking matters too. AI dramatically reduces the cost of producing code, but that doesn't automatically mean better outcomes. The bottleneck shifts: if code is cheap, then the quality, security, and review of that code becomes the constraint. Theory of constraints applies here just as it does anywhere else.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          The specific tools will change. The principles of how you use them won't.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* What I've Built / Researched */}
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary mb-4">What I've Built & Researched</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Led a comprehensive research programme for a UK government department evaluating AI-powered security testing tools for air-gapped environments. Analysed 60+ solutions across the market, developed a structured evaluation framework, and authored a 50+ page technical report.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Senior stakeholders called it "a magnificent piece of work" and "exactly what I wanted to get out of it."
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* Where This Is Going */}
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary mb-4">Where This Is Going</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          These are the very early days. The tools will change, the capabilities will grow, and the patterns of how humans and AI collaborate will evolve. What won't change is the need for experienced engineers who understand the fundamentals: how to break problems down, how to verify solutions, how to think about security and quality, and how to make good trade-offs.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          After 25 years of paradigm shifts, the calibrated view is: be genuinely excited about the possibilities, be honest about the limitations, and keep a close eye on which principles endure.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-8">
        <a href="/" class="text-sm text-accent-functional hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">&larr; Back to main</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
